# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. Nothing here is required to run the site
# locally — every value has a working fallback — but the waitlist silently
# logs instead of mailing until RESEND_API_KEY is set.

# The canonical origin, used for metadataBase, OG image URLs, the sitemap and
# robots.txt. Unset, these fall back to the production domain — which is wrong
# on a preview deployment, and used to happen silently. A build with CI=true
# and this unset now fails rather than publishing production URLs from a
# non-production deploy. `next dev` and a local `next build` still use the
# fallback.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://sidbuilds.in

# Waitlist mail (components/home/StoreWaitlist/submit.ts).
# Required for a submission to actually leave the server; without it the entry
# is written to the server log in development and dropped in production.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Optional. Defaults are in submit.ts.
WAITLIST_TO=
WAITLIST_FROM=

# Where the site reports Core Web Vitals and unhandled errors. Optional, and
# unset means nothing is ever sent — see lib/telemetry.ts. Any endpoint that
# accepts a JSON POST will do; the payload carries no identifier of any kind.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TELEMETRY_URL=

# Reactions and comments on a case study (lib/upstash.ts).
#
# In production these are injected automatically by the Upstash database added
# through the Vercel Marketplace — nothing needs pasting, which is the reason
# to add it that way. `lib/upstash.ts` also accepts the KV_REST_API_* pair,
# because some marketplace flows still use Vercel KV's names for the same two
# values.
#
# Locally you can leave both blank: `next dev` falls back to an in-process
# store (lib/upstashDev.ts) so the like, the comment box and the rate limits
# all work, and everything empties on restart. To develop against real data,
# copy the two values out of the Upstash console into `.env.local`. Unset,
# every study still renders and the engagement block says plainly that it is
# not connected rather than showing zeroes.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# Optional. Salts the hash that stands in for a visitor's identity, so no store
# on this site holds an IP address. Used by both the engagement store (who has
# already liked what) and the footer's live count (who is on the site now) —
# see lib/visitorId.ts. Changing it resets who has already reacted; it does
# not reset the counts.
#
# The footer's visitor total needs no configuration of its own: it lives in the
# same Redis as the comments, under one key, and shows an em dash when there is
# none. Note that it does NOT identify a visitor this way — a total that runs
# for years cannot use an address, so the browser mints and keeps its own id.
# See lib/visitorStore.ts. The salt is used for its per-address rate limit.
ENGAGEMENT_SALT=

# The Fantasy card's live data (lib/fantasy.ts).
#
# NEITHER OF THESE IS A CREDENTIAL. The three FPL endpoints the card reads are
# public and unauthenticated; `FPL_ENTRY_ID` is the manager number out of the
# URL of your own points page (fantasy.premierleague.com/entry/<this>/event/3),
# which anyone who looks at your team can already see. They live here rather
# than in the repo because they are Siddhant's, not because they are secret.
#
# Unset, the card is still live in the half that needs no identity: the
# fixture, the crests, the score and whether the match is being played all come
# off the wire, and only the three gameweek scores fall back to the values
# written down in content/site.ts. The overall rank is hidden rather than
# faked. The whole card falls back if the API itself is unreachable, so a build
# with no network still succeeds.
FPL_ENTRY_ID=

# Optional. A club's FPL short name — mun, ars, liv, che, mci — to follow that
# team through the season instead. Unset, the card shows the biggest match of
# the current gameweek: a live one if there is one, otherwise the next to kick
# off, otherwise the last one played.
FPL_CLUB=

# The music card's listening data (lib/lastfm.ts, lib/nowPlaying.ts).
#
# NEITHER OF THESE IS A CREDENTIAL EITHER. Last.fm's read methods take an API
# key and nothing else — no OAuth, no refresh, no expiry — and a listening
# history on a public profile is already public. They live here because they
# are Siddhant's, not because they are secret.
#
# Get a key at last.fm/api/account/create. The user is the profile whose
# scrobbles the card reads, and it only has scrobbles if something is sending
# them: Spotify connects natively under Last.fm's Settings -> Applications, and
# the Last.fm app scrobbles Apple Music.
#
# Why Last.fm and not Spotify or Apple Music directly: Spotify removed the
# 30-second preview from its Web API in November 2024, so it can say what was
# played but cannot play it, and Apple's personalised endpoints need a user
# token that only a browser can mint and that expires twice a year. Neither is
# a thing a server route can hold on its own. See the header of lib/lastfm.ts.
#
# Unset, the card renders its unavailable state — no track, transport disabled
# — and the build still succeeds. The audio and the artwork come from Apple's
# iTunes Search API, which needs no key at all, so there is nothing to set for
# those.
LASTFM_API_KEY=
LASTFM_USER=

# Optional. The iTunes storefront to search, as a two-letter country code.
# Defaults to US. It changes the answer materially — the same query returns
# five genuine Strokes tracks in IN and none in US — so set it to the
# storefront whose catalogue matches the listening.
ITUNES_COUNTRY=
